WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:10.600
USB and removable media analysis combines safe evidence handling, host artifact review, file system analysis, timeline correlation, and careful interpretation.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:22.100
The media itself may contain important files, but the host systems often explain when the device appeared, which user context was active, and whether there are signs of file interaction or transfer.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.960
Strong investigations begin with preservation.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.620
Unknown media should not be casually connected to production systems.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:39.960
Investigators should document condition, apply appropriate write protection where available, create forensic images or verified working copies, hash evidence, maintain chain of custody, and preserve original media.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.860
These steps make later analysis more repeatable and defensible.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.320
Device identity requires caution.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:53.740
Volume labels, serial numbers, controller identifiers, file systems, drive letters, and host records may not all agree.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:59.240
A device may be reformatted, used through an adapter, or reported differently across systems.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:07.380
Identity findings are strongest when multiple details align across physical evidence, media metadata, and host artifacts.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.720
Analysis should separate observations from conclusions.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.220
Device connection is not automatically file transfer.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.400
File presence is not automatically user intent.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:25.700
Timestamps are important but can be affected by file system behavior, copying, time zones, clock drift, application behavior, or tool presentation.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.280
Timelines should include corroboration and uncertainty notes.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:37.340
The goal is to preserve evidence, reconstruct relevant activity, document uncertainty, and support defensible investigative findings.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:48.840
Good removable media analysis is practical and careful: it protects original evidence, uses multiple sources, avoids unsafe handling, and communicates conclusions in proportion to the artifacts.
